WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.320
Netflix is streaming the SAG Awards&nbsp;
on its YouTube channel on Sunday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.960
February 26. The Screen Actors Guild ceremony&nbsp;
is usually a great predictor for the Oscars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.640
but not always. You may remember some of&nbsp;
these famous performances that got snubbed.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.600
Cary Joji Fukunaga's 2015 film Beasts of No&nbsp;
Nation, a drama about child soldiers in West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.120
Africa, has the distinction of being Netflix's&nbsp;
first-ever original feature film. It's possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.040
that Academy members were resistant to awarding&nbsp;
a streaming release, which could explain why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.480
critically-acclaimed film didn't receive a&nbsp;
single Oscar nomination despite significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.400
awards success elsewhere — particularly for&nbsp;
Idris Elba's performance as the Commandant.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.620
"All of you that have never been listened&nbsp;
to before and have seen your family killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.424
you know have something that stands for you."

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:54.300
Of course, another factor could also explain&nbsp;
the snub. CNN blamed it on the Academy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.340
racism. 2016 was the second year in a row&nbsp;
where all the acting nominees were white,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.255
resulting in the #OscarsSoWhite controversy.

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:06.060
"Some people are born to be leaders. Others are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.909
born to be followers. And others&nbsp;
are just waiting to be dying."

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.740
Elba, SAG's only Black nominee that year,&nbsp;
won the guild's best supporting actor award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.340
despite his Oscar snub, while&nbsp;
the Oscar for best supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.120
actor that year ultimately went to&nbsp;
Mark Rylance for Bridge of Spies.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:30.180
Emily Blunt’s performance as Evelyn Abbot&nbsp;
in the 2018 horror blockbuster A Quiet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.200
Place earned her a SAG award win, but she&nbsp;
was snubbed of an Oscar nomination. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.260
Academy doesn't always recognize&nbsp;
great acting in horror movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.562
but A Quiet Place was such a hit that it seemed&nbsp;
like Blunt had a genuine shot at a nomination.

00:01:39.843 --> 00:01:42.192
"No, she was with me. She was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.192 --> 00:01:48.300
with me and then I had to go and do laundry&nbsp;
and he was with you. How did they – ?"

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.460
Category confusion may have been another&nbsp;
obstacle for Blunt. She and co-star/director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.380
John Krasinski were originally pitched in the&nbsp;
lead acting categories but switched to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.300
supporting columns midway through campaign&nbsp;
season. Blunt won the 2018 SAG award for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.740
best supporting actress, which speaks to&nbsp;
SAG's own snubs that year. Regina King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.760
heartbreaking as Sharon Rivers in If&nbsp;
Beale Street Could Talk, was a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.949
deserving Oscar winner for best supporting&nbsp;
actress but wasn't nominated by the Guild.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.860
There are debates to be had over who most&nbsp;
deserved the best actor Oscar in 2021. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.120
posthumous SAG winner Chadwick Boseman as&nbsp;
musician Levee Green in Ma Rainey's Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.500
Bottom and Oscar winner Anthony Hopkins&nbsp;
as dementia patient Anthony in The Father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.000
delivered incredible performances.&nbsp;
However, the way the Oscars telecast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.436
handled Hopkins' victory places this among the&nbsp;
most egregious snubs in recent Oscar history.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:46.680
"All right, Cutler. Straighten that out."

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.580
Boseman was seen as such a sure bet&nbsp;
to win that Oscar attendees were given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.440
NFTs of his face. Whereas best picture is&nbsp;
traditionally the final award of the night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.820
this time best actor was saved for&nbsp;
last, presumably to send viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.140
off on the emotional high of the late Black&nbsp;
Panther star's family accepting the award.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.200
"I got talent. Me and this horn, we's tight. If my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.260
daddy woulda known I was gonna turn out&nbsp;
like this, he woulda named me Gabriel."

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.160
When Boseman didn't win, foiling the&nbsp;
send-off the show had seemingly planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.760
there was serious backlash. To&nbsp;
make matters even more awkward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.080
Hopkins wasn't there, given that&nbsp;
it was 4 AM in his native Wales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:28.140
and the Academy wouldn't let him accept the award&nbsp;
via Zoom, despite the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.780
The Screen Actors Guild members are much bigger&nbsp;
fans of Viola Davis than the Academy is. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.920
groups awarded her their best supporting&nbsp;
actress awards for playing Rose Lee Maxson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.380
in Denzel Washington's 2016 adaptation of&nbsp;
the August Wilson play Fences. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.900
Davis has yet to win a best actress Oscar,&nbsp;
despite having SAG awards for playing Aibileen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:54.118
Clark in 2011's The Help and the title&nbsp;
role in 2020's Ma Rainey's Black Bottom.

00:03:54.118 --> 00:04:00.538
"You is kind. You is smart. You is important."

00:04:00.538 --> 00:04:05.100
Davis' performance in The Help lost to Meryl&nbsp;
Streep, who won her third Oscar for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:10.620
Margaret Thatcher in the otherwise mediocre The&nbsp;
Iron Lady. Davis in Ma Rainey's Black Bottom lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.867
to another three-time Oscar winner, Frances&nbsp;
McDormand, for her performance in Nomadland.

00:04:15.867 --> 00:04:19.450
"I don't care what nobody else do. Ma&nbsp;
was the first. Don't you forget it."

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:23.460
It's worth noting that the Screen Actors&nbsp;
Guild is one of many awards bodies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.860
nominate Davis for playing General Nanisca&nbsp;
in Gina Prince-Bythewood's 2021 historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:34.169
epic The Woman King. But The Woman King&nbsp;
failed to garner a single Oscar nomination.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:39.300
So why did Meryl Streep win for The Iron&nbsp;
Lady? Well, the film had the backing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.040
Harvey Weinstein, who was at the height of his&nbsp;
ability to bulldoze his way into most major Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.840
races. A more charitable read of the situation,&nbsp;
however, is that Oscar voters felt Streep was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.060
robbed three years earlier for her performance&nbsp;
in Doubt, John Patrick Shanley's adaptation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.920
his Tony-winning play. Sister Aloysius is&nbsp;
among the richest roles Streep has played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.340
and would have made a more deserving&nbsp;
third Oscar win for the legendary actress.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:03.900
"I can fight you."

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.120
"You will lose."

00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:09.960
The performance that beat Streep's for&nbsp;
the best actress Oscar that year was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.400
also a SAG Award-winning one — just in a&nbsp;
different category. Kate Winslet's work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.300
as the Nazi concentration camp guard&nbsp;
Hanna Schmitz in The Reader earned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.680
best supporting actress nod from SAG but&nbsp;
was considered a lead role by the Academy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.320
and ultimately won that category. In retrospect,&nbsp;
it doesn't look great that a movie that tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.760
sell an extremely questionable relationship&nbsp;
between a teenage boy and a grown woman as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.719
a "very genuine love story" beat out a&nbsp;
movie about standing up to child abusers.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.960
If Streep's 2012 Oscar victory over Davis was&nbsp;
due to people feeling she was owed for Doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.240
then Winslet's 2008 campaign beat out Streep&nbsp;
in large part due to the sense that she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.680
owed for her whole career up to that point. The&nbsp;
Reader was Winslet's sixth Oscar nomination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.280
and many felt she should have won&nbsp;
for one of her earlier nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.900
roles such as Clementine Kruczynski in&nbsp;
Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:06.135
Rose DeWitt Bukater in Titanic, or&nbsp;
Marianne Dashwood in Sense and Sensibility.

00:06:06.135 --> 00:06:11.675
"Love is not love which alters&nbsp;
when it alteration finds."

00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:15.360
Winslet's acclaimed performance as&nbsp;
the hyper-emotional middle child in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.580
Ang Lee's 1995 Jane Austen adaptation&nbsp;
was only her second feature film role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.200
following her debut in Peter Jackson's true&nbsp;
crime drama Heavenly Creatures the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.580
year. Winslet's best supporting actress&nbsp;
victory at the age of 20 made her the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.500
youngest SAG Award winner in history. At the&nbsp;
Oscars, however, Winslet ended up losing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:39.284
Mira Sorvino in the middle-of-the-road&nbsp;
Woody Allen flick Mighty Aphrodite.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:45.000
Ties can happen at the Oscars, but they're&nbsp;
exceedingly rare. It's only happened six times in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.880
Academy Awards history. One wonders how close the&nbsp;
Academy was to another tie at the 1998 ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.300
the year the SAG Awards had its first and&nbsp;
thus far only tie in the best supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:59.460
actress race. The dual SAG honors went to Kim&nbsp;
Basinger as Lynn Bracken in L.A. Confidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:05.220
and Gloria Stuart as Rose Dawson Calvert in&nbsp;
Titanic. Basinger ultimately took the Oscar.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.460
Stuart, who played Rose as an&nbsp;
old woman, and Kate Winslet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.360
who played Rose for the majority of the&nbsp;
film, both lost their respective Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.580
races. Basinger won for skillfully evoking&nbsp;
Old Hollywood glamor in a gritty neo-noir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:23.520
but Stuart, who'd been acting since the 1930s,&nbsp;
was actual Old Hollywood glamor! Though awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.152
ties are statistical anomalies, in this&nbsp;
case, maybe it would have been justified.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.200
In 1996, the Oscars and SAG Awards didn't agree on&nbsp;
who gave the most worthy supporting performances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.420
of the year. Not only did Winslet win best&nbsp;
supporting actress at SAG yet lose at the Oscars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:44.700
but Ed Harris as NASA Flight Director Gene&nbsp;
Kranz in Apollo 13 won best supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.980
actor at SAG only for the Academy to prefer&nbsp;
Kevin Spacey in The Usual Suspects. When it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.160
comes to the question of "Which performance&nbsp;
would you actually want to watch today?",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.520
it goes without saying that&nbsp;
Harris easily beats Spacey.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.680
"We never lost an American in space. We're sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.520
as hell not gonna lose one on my&nbsp;
watch. Failure is not an option."

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.600
But even if we ignore all of the sordid&nbsp;
details we now know about Spacey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.860
Harris would have been a worthy Oscar winner&nbsp;
for his powerful portrayal of heroism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.960
professionalism under extreme pressure. This&nbsp;
was Harris' first Oscar nomination. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.780
later nominated for best supporting actor for&nbsp;
1998's The Truman Show and 2002's The Hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.760
as well as best actor for 2000's Pollock.&nbsp;
He's one of those actors who's been doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.229
such great work for so long that it's&nbsp;
strange that he hasn't yet won an Oscar.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:35.460
Two of the strongest performances in&nbsp;
Peter Jackson's Lord of the Rings trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:41.100
Andy Serkis as Smeagol/Gollum and Sean Astin&nbsp;
as Samwise Gamgee, weren't nominated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.920
Academy or SAG — though SAG did nominate all three&nbsp;
films and award Return of the King best ensemble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:52.200
The questions of whether motion capture acting&nbsp;
"counts" likely killed Serkis' awards chances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:57.360
and the general ensemble nature of the trilogy&nbsp;
made singling out individual performers difficult.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.140
The one individual performance in&nbsp;
the trilogy to get significant awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.520
recognition was Sir Ian McKellen as the&nbsp;
wizard Gandalf the Grey in the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.500
2001's The Fellowship of the Ring. He won&nbsp;
best supporting actor at the SAG Awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.480
and was nominated in the same category at the&nbsp;
Oscars, ultimately losing to Jim Broadbent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.220
Iris. Had McKellen won, he would have been the&nbsp;
first openly gay man to win an acting Oscar.

00:09:20.306 --> 00:09:28.826
"A wizard is never late, Frodo Baggins. Nor is&nbsp;
he early. He arrives precisely when he means to."

00:09:28.826 --> 00:09:33.420
Comparisons can be drawn between McKellen's&nbsp;
nomination and Alec Guinness' nomination for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.280
original Star Wars. The Academy might not take&nbsp;
acting in fantasy movies seriously very often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.680
but when they do, they seem drawn to wizened&nbsp;
mentor figures played by beloved older actors.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:50.520
Martin Scorsese's 2002 historical crime epic&nbsp;
Gangs of New York was nominated for 10 Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:57.060
Awards but didn't win any of them. In Oscars&nbsp;
history, only 1977's The Turning Point and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:03.000
1985's The Color Purple had more nominations –&nbsp;
11 – without a win. Gangs of New York is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.220
Scorsese's more divisive films. It seemed to have&nbsp;
the Academy's respect but not much of its love.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:12.900
Even critics who weren't crazy about the film&nbsp;
as a whole, however, were enamored with Daniel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:18.929
Day-Lewis' performance as the xenophobic gang&nbsp;
leader William Cutting, aka "Bill the Butcher."

00:10:18.929 --> 00:10:26.400
"I defeat my enemy. I extinguish his life.&nbsp;
I consume him as I consume these flames."

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.680
Ireland's favorite method actor eventually won&nbsp;
Oscars for similarly transformative performances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:38.460
as Daniel Plainview in 2007's There Will Be&nbsp;
Blood and as Abraham Lincoln in 2012's Lincoln,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:44.820
in addition to his 1990 Oscar for My Left Foot,&nbsp;
in which he played a man with cerebral palsy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:50.280
has only the use of his left foot. The best actor&nbsp;
Oscar that year ultimately went to Adrien Brody in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:55.776
The Pianist, a film which remains controversial&nbsp;
due to the actions of director Roman Polanski.

00:10:56.094 --> 00:11:00.720
Johnny Depp may be a polarizing figure, but&nbsp;
it's difficult to argue against the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.840
that his performance as Captain Jack&nbsp;
Sparrow in Pirates of the Caribbean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.980
The Curse of the Black Pearl, was a defining&nbsp;
moment in a career that has gotten bogged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:12.600
down in repetitive collaborations with Tim&nbsp;
Burton that yield ever-diminishing returns.

00:11:12.816 --> 00:11:15.756
"You are without doubt the worst&nbsp;
pirate I have ever heard of."&nbsp;

00:11:16.711 --> 00:11:18.091
"But you have heard of me."

00:11:18.091 --> 00:11:22.260
The idea that Pirates of the Caribbean: The&nbsp;
Curse of the Black Pearl, a movie based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:26.580
a Disneyland attraction, could be in serious&nbsp;
awards contention at all – let alone against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.280
Sean Penn in Mystic River and Bill Murray in Lost&nbsp;
in Translation – is entirely due to the incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.380
job Depp did as Captain Jack Sparrow. All the more&nbsp;
impressive that he got in on the strength of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:43.088
chaotic comedic energy, given that awards bodies&nbsp;
far too often overlook comedic performances.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:48.780
On paper, Eddie Murphy's performance as soul&nbsp;
singer Jimmy "Thunder" Early in Dreamgirls, Bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:55.260
Condon's 2006 film adaptation of the hit Broadway&nbsp;
musical, seemed like a lock for the Oscar. It had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.260 --> 00:12:01.020
everything Academy voters love: a beloved comedic&nbsp;
actor taking on a serious role, a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:06.900
inspired by a real person, a drug addiction story,&nbsp;
and the revelation of unexpected musical talent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:12.360
And Murphy was great in the role, stealing every&nbsp;
scene Jennifer Hudson hadn't already stolen.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:17.280
Murphy won the SAG Award for best supporting&nbsp;
actor, as well as the Golden Globe and Critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.620
Choice Award. He ultimately lost the Oscar to&nbsp;
Alan Arkin as grandpa Edwin Hoover in Little Miss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:28.560
Sunshine. How did Arkin win? Affection for Little&nbsp;
Miss Sunshine as a whole definitely helped; the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:34.980
indie comedy was nominated for best picture, while&nbsp;
Dreamgirls wasn't. Then there's the Norbit factor.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.620
“Don’t you think I realize&nbsp;
what’s going on here, miss.”

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:42.420
Starring in a terrible movie while campaigning&nbsp;
for an Oscar didn't help Murphy's chances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:46.320
although Norbit snagged an Oscar nomination&nbsp;
for best makeup the following year.

